<Base title="Retention and deletion" description="Trace data through related records, caches, reports and retained backups, then make deletion replayable after a restore.">
 <meta slot="head" name="robots" content="noindex" />
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage · Working draft</p><h1>Retention and deletion</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational operations</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/">Relational introduction ↗</a></div></header>
  <p>A record has a life beyond its first table. A customer profile can acquire related notes, appear in a report, be copied into search, enter an export and remain in a backup after disappearing from the live service. Deleting the profile row changes one of those places. It does not automatically change all of them.</p>
  <p>A retention policy defines which data remains useful, for how long and for which purpose. A deletion process applies that policy to the records and copies the system actually keeps. The engineering question is concrete: what disappears now, what remains inaccessible until its retained copy expires, and what prevents old data from being reintroduced?</p>
  <p>We will remove a customer and their private notes, then follow an older copy through recovery. The example chooses to delete those records completely from the live application. Real retention periods and exceptions need an established organisational policy; the mechanics below do not choose a legal deadline.</p>
  <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#scope">The deletion’s scope</a></li><li><a href="#live">Deleting related records</a></li><li><a href="#copies">Other live copies</a></li><li><a href="#restore">Restores and reintroduction</a></li><li><a href="#retention">Expiring retained data</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="scope"><h2>The deletion’s scope</h2>
   <p>Our live database has two customers: Ada, ID 1, and Lin, ID 2. Ada has note 10, “Call at noon”; Lin has note 20, “Use front desk”. The notes reference customer IDs with foreign keys. A retained snapshot was taken before Ada’s deletion and contains both customer profiles.</p>
   <p>For this exercise, the deletion includes Ada’s profile and notes. It leaves Lin alone. A minimal deletion ledger records Ada’s stable customer ID and the date of the decision, so recovery can apply that decision again. The ledger does not retain the deleted name or note.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Data copies and their treatment"><table><thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Contains Ada?</th><th>Required action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Customer profile</td><td>ID and name</td><td>Remove from live application</td></tr><tr><td>Private note</td><td>ID and free text</td><td>Remove dependent record</td></tr><tr><td>Deletion ledger</td><td>Stable ID and decision date</td><td>Protect and retain for the replay window</td></tr><tr><td>Older retained snapshot</td><td>Earlier profile</td><td>Restrict access, expire under policy and suppress on restore</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>A real inventory also names derived stores, exports, attachments, logs and processors outside the application database. Follow both explicit foreign keys and informal references such as a customer ID embedded in JSON or a file path. The database can only enforce relationships it knows about.</p>
   <p>Some systems mark a row with <code>deleted_at</code> first. That can hide it from normal queries or coordinate a staged workflow, but the fields still exist. Call it logical deletion and define which readers must exclude it. If the policy requires removal of the personal fields, setting a flag alone has not completed that operation.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="live"><h2>Deleting related records</h2>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=deletion-and-copies">deletion-and-copies lesson</a> creates these records in independent <code>lifecycle_</code> tables. You send the deletion worker’s commands on one connection. First try removing Ada’s parent row:</p>
   <Sql code="DELETE FROM lifecycle_customers WHERE customer_id = 1;" label="Attempt the incomplete deletion" />
   <p>PostgreSQL rejects it with SQLSTATE <code>23503</code>: note 10 still references Ada. This is useful protection against a dangling reference, but the foreign key does not choose the retention policy for that note. We must decide whether to delete it, move it to an allowed historical representation or refuse the parent deletion.</p>
   <p>Here the policy says to remove both. The worker records the deletion identity and removes the dependent and parent records in one transaction:</p>
   <Sql code={lesson.steps[2].sql} label="Record the deletion and remove the live customer and note together" />
   <p>The resulting counts are one live customer, one note and two saved customer rows. Lin and her note survive. The ledger and live removal commit together, so a failed transaction cannot claim completion while leaving Ada’s live profile unchanged.</p>
   <p><code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> could encode the decision to delete notes automatically when a customer is deleted. Use it when every child’s lifecycle genuinely follows the parent. Do not choose it merely to make the error disappear: a cascade through a large relationship tree can remove much more than the initiating statement appears to name. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-constraints.html#DDL-CONSTRAINTS-FK">foreign-key documentation</a> describes the available actions.</p>
   <p>For a large deletion, one transaction may create too much lock, log and cleanup work. A resumable job can remove bounded batches, but intermediate states then become visible. Mark the subject unavailable to ordinary operations, prevent new dependent data from being created, track progress and acknowledge completion only after the required stages finish. A status field is coordination here, not a substitute for eventual removal.</p>
   <p>In PostgreSQL, deleting a row also differs from overwriting its bytes immediately. Older row versions can remain while snapshots need them; vacuum later makes eligible space reusable. Backups, WAL archives and storage snapshots follow their own retention. Do not promise physical erasure merely because a SELECT no longer finds the row.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="copies"><h2>Other live copies</h2>
   <p>Suppose Ada’s name also appears in a customer search index. The relational transaction cannot directly make that separate service commit at the same instant. The deletion worker needs durable progress for that destination: which identity to remove, whether removal was acknowledged and whether retry is still needed.</p>
   <p>One arrangement writes a deletion event or outbox record in the same database transaction as the live removal. A delivery worker then retries sending it until the destination has applied it. The destination should treat repeated deletion of the same identity as harmless. The event describes a durable decision; deleting the source row without preserving how to tell other systems can leave those copies behind indefinitely.</p>
   <p>The destination must also handle ordering. A delayed “customer updated” event from before the deletion could arrive afterwards and recreate the profile. Associate updates and deletions with an identity and ordering rule the destination can enforce, or retain a suppression record that rejects obsolete reintroduction. Simply sending a DELETE once does not settle later arrivals.</p>
   <p>Caches need invalidation or an expiry bound that satisfies the chosen policy. Analytical stores and materialized reports may require refreshed or recomputed results. Exports need named owners and retention rules because an unmanaged downloaded file cannot be recalled by changing the source table. Free-text logs and support notes can contain values outside the structured customer columns.</p>
   <p>Report completion in terms of these destinations. “Removed from the live database; search removal pending” is a different state from “every required active copy removed”. The distinction helps the operator find unfinished work and prevents an early success response from hiding a failed downstream step.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="restore"><h2>Restores and reintroduction</h2>
   <p>The saved snapshot still contains Ada. The lesson models recovery by inserting its missing customer rows back into the live table. Ada reappears even though her original live deletion succeeded. This is a table-copy model, not a backup restore test, but it exposes the consequence of recovering a point before the deletion.</p>
   <p>Before reopening a real restored service, obtain the deletion decisions that happened after its recovery point and apply them to the restored data. Those decisions must survive independently of the old copy. A ledger restored from that same old snapshot would be missing the very decisions we need.</p>
   <Sql code={lesson.steps[4].sql} label="Reapply preserved deletion decisions before exposing restored data" />
   <p>The first query checks for restored customers whose IDs are still listed for deletion. It returns no rows. The second shows only Lin. Repeating the replay has the same result: deleting an already absent row is harmless. The lesson lets you restore the saved rows again and repeat the suppression.</p>
   <p>On a real service, keep the target isolated while applying this procedure across customer records, dependants and derived stores. Disable outbound messages and ingestion that could reconstruct removed profiles. Validate suppression before routing users or background jobs to it. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">recovery article</a> places these checks alongside other restore validation.</p>
   <p>The ledger itself needs a policy. Stable identity is useful for matching old copies, but it may still be identifying information and should have restricted access. Retain only what replay requires, protect it from ordinary application deletion and establish how long it must survive. Reusing a deleted customer ID for another person would make replay ambiguous, so identities used for this purpose must not be reassigned.</p>
   <p>A suppression record can eventually expire only when the copies and delayed inputs it protects against can no longer reintroduce that identity, according to the system’s defined recovery and ingestion windows. Keeping it forever without a reason is not a retention design; removing it before the oldest recoverable copy expires loses the protection.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="retention"><h2>Expiring retained data</h2>
   <p>Time-based data often has a simpler bulk boundary. If operational events are retained for an agreed period and partitioned by event date, a whole expired partition may be removable without deleting its rows individually. That can reduce row-by-row work, but dropping or detaching the partition still has locking, dependency and downstream-copy consequences.</p>
   <p>Check the partition’s actual bounds and the policy’s time meaning. A partition containing events from 1–31 March may be eligible only when every record in that range is expired. If retention is defined from ingestion time rather than event time, a late-arriving March event changes the reasoning. Likewise, a partition per month cannot efficiently erase one customer’s scattered records merely by dropping a month.</p>
   <p>Detaching a partition removes it from queries through the parent, but leaves a separate table containing its data. Treat archival, detachment and deletion as distinct actions with explicit destinations. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-partitioning.html#DDL-PARTITIONING-MAINTENANCE">partition maintenance</a> documentation explains the operations and lock differences.</p>
   <p>For retained backups, define access restrictions and expiry with the recovery requirement. Record which backup generations and archives can still contain a removed record, when they expire and how restoration applies later deletions. Erasing a backup immediately may destroy required recovery coverage; silently retaining it forever defeats the stated lifecycle. The policy needs an explicit resolution rather than leaving that tradeoff to a deletion SQL statement.</p>
   <p>The completed operation is therefore more than a row count. Ada’s profile and note are gone from live use; active copies have acknowledged removal or are visibly pending; retained copies have a bounded policy; and a restore cannot reopen her profile without replaying the deletion decision. Those are properties a team can test and operate.</p>
  </section>
